\begin_layout Title
Econometrics I: Final Exam Solutions (2012)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a standard linear regression model 
\begin_inset Formula $y=X\beta+u$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is a 
\emph on
strictly
\emph default
 exogenous 
\begin_inset Formula $N\times K$
\end_inset

 matrix of regressors with full column rank.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
What is the critical assumption for the OLSE 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 to be unbiased? Prove its unbiasedness.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Give a name of a model for which the OLSE 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is biased even if the assumption in (a) is satisfied.
 Just the name of the model, and nothing else.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The critical assumptions for the OLSE 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 to be unbiased are
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}\ X=K$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is unique.
 Also, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}(X,y)=K+1$
\end_inset

 prevents a perfect fit.
 Under this assumption,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}=(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}y
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $E(u|X)=0$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 is unbasied conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(\hat{\beta}|X) & = & E\left[(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}y|X\right]\\
 & = & E\left[\beta+(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}u|X\right]\\
 & = & \beta+(X^{\prime}X)^{-1}X^{\prime}E(u|X)\\
 & = & \beta
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Censored Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Explain the basic ideas of four different estimation methods: LSE, MLE,
 MME, and MELE.
 Make your answers as brief as possible without dropping the core ideas.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
LSE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A residual is the difference between the observed value 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}$
\end_inset

 and the predicted value 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 We wish to minimize the distance between 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{i}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the LSE finds an estimator that minimizes the sum of squared residuals,
 i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
SSR(\tilde{\beta})=(y-X\tilde{\beta})^{\prime}(y-X\tilde{\beta})
\]

\end_inset

No other informaiton is required
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
MLE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The joint pdf of a random sample tells us the probability of a specific
 set of observations, given the distribution paramaeters.
 Assuming observations are i.i.d., the joint pdf is written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(y_{1},\ldots,y_{n};\theta)={\displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^{n}}f(y_{i};\theta)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{i};\theta)$
\end_inset

 is the pdf of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 In estimation, we do not know the distribution parameters, but we have
 a set of observations.
 So, we wish to find the parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 that could have generated this set of observations, most likely.
 Thus, to find 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

, we maximize the log-likelihood function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ln\left[f(y_{1},\ldots,y_{n};\theta)\right]={\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\ln\left[f(y_{i};\theta)\right]
\]

\end_inset

To perform MLE, we require knowledge of the population distribution function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
MME
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The basic approach of the method of moments is to set the sample moments
 of lowest order with the moments of the population distribution, and then
 solve for unknown parameters.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(X-\theta) & = & 0\\
n^{-1}{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}X_{i}-\theta & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\bar under
MELE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose that we do not have the complete information contained in the distributi
on funciton, but have only information about the moments.
 The MELE wishes to maximize empirical probabilities replacing the density
 function with moment conditions as constraints.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max_{p_{i}} & {\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\ln(p_{i})\\
\mbox{s.t.} & {\displaystyle p_{i}>0}\\
 & {\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}p_{i}=1\\
 & {\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}p_{i}(y_{i}-\theta)=0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Note that 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}p_{i}=1$
\end_inset

 is a normalization, and 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}p_{i}(y_{i}-\theta)=0$
\end_inset

 is the sample moment condition with the empirical probability 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}$
\end_inset

 in place of 
\begin_inset Formula $(\nicefrac{1}{n})$
\end_inset

.
 First, taking the parameters as given, we estimate the log-likelihood function
 with sample moment conditions.
 Then, we estimate parameters from the empirical log-likelihood function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
A decision maker 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 faces a choice problem: picking one from three choices.
 Let the stochastic utility of each choice 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 be denoted by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
U_{ij}=X_{i}\beta_{j}+Z_{ij}\gamma+\epsilon_{ij}=m_{ij}+\epsilon_{ij},\ j=1,\ldots,J;\ i=1,\ldots,n
\]

\end_inset

When alternative 
\begin_inset Formula $j$
\end_inset

 is chosen by individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, we observe 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{ij}=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{ik}=0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $k\neq j$
\end_inset

.
 We wish to analyze the probability 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{ij}\equiv P(y_{ij}=1)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Show the steps to derive 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{ij}$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{ij}$
\end_inset

 is an i.i.d.
 random variable with a common cdf 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\epsilon_{ij})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose error terms are i.i.d.
 Gumbell variables.
 What is the model called in each case specified below:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}=0\ \forall\ j$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma\neq0,\ \beta_{j}\neq0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
If error terms are i.i.d.
 Gumbell variables,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{ij}=\frac{e^{m_{ij}}}{{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}e^{m_{ik}}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Some of the parameters are not identifiable.
 Show which parameters are not identifiable, and what is the convential
 normalization rule?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Explain how to construct the likelihood function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
In the model (ii) of question (b), what does 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{jk}$
\end_inset

 represent? Show your work.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
In the model (i) of question (b), show and explain the independence of irrelevan
t alternatives (IIA).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Which assumption of the model leads to IIA? Explain briefuly a model that
 avoids that assumption.
 Just two or three sentences with no equations.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{ij}=1$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{ij}>U_{ik}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $k\neq j$
\end_inset

.
 Thus
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
p_{ij}\equiv P(y_{ij}=1) & = & P(U_{ij}>U_{ik}\ \forall\ k\neq j)\\
 & = & P(m_{ij}+\epsilon_{ij}>m_{ik}+\epsilon_{ik}\ \forall\ k\neq j)\\
 & = & P(\epsilon_{ik}<(m_{ij}-m_{ik})+\epsilon_{ij}\ \forall\ k\neq j)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

We can find this joint distribution when we consider the conditional density
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{ik}$
\end_inset

 for a given 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{ij}$
\end_inset

, and then compute the weighted average of the conditional probability with
 weights equal to the probability of 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{ij}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(y_{ij}=1)={\displaystyle \int}P(\epsilon_{ik}<(m_{ij}-m_{ik})+\epsilon_{ij}|\epsilon_{ij};\forall\ k\neq j)\cdot f(\epsilon_{ij})\ d\epsilon_{ij}
\]

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{ij}$
\end_inset

 are i.i.d.
 with a cdf 
\begin_inset Formula $F(\epsilon_{ij})$
\end_inset

, then the conditional probability is the same as marginal probability,
 and the joint probability is the product of marginal probabilities.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(\epsilon_{ik}<(m_{ij}-m_{ik})+\epsilon_{ij}|\epsilon_{ij};\forall\ k\neq j)={\displaystyle \prod_{k\neq j}}F((m_{ij}-m_{ik})+\epsilon_{ij})
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(y_{ij}=1)={\displaystyle \int}\left[{\displaystyle \prod_{k\neq j}}F((m_{ij}-m_{ik})+\epsilon_{ij})\right]f(\epsilon_{ij})\ d\epsilon_{ij}
\]

\end_inset


\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(i) conditional logit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(ii) multinomial logit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(iii) mixed logit
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part C, (i)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{j}$
\end_inset

 are not identifiable.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{ij}=\frac{e^{m_{ij}}}{{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}e^{m_{ik}}}=\frac{e^{X_{i}\beta_{j}+Z_{ij}\gamma}}{{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}e^{X_{i}\beta_{k}+Z_{ik}\gamma}}
\]

\end_inset

Now, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\beta}_{j}=\beta_{j}+\alpha$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tilde{p}_{ij}=\frac{e^{m_{ij}}}{{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}e^{m_{ik}}}=\frac{e^{X_{i}\beta_{j}+X_{i}\alpha+Z_{ij}\gamma}}{{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}e^{X_{i}\beta_{k}+X_{i}\alpha+Z_{ik}\gamma}}=\frac{e^{X_{i}\beta_{j}+Z_{ij}\gamma}}{{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}e^{X_{i}\beta_{k}+Z_{ik}\gamma}}=p_{ij}
\]

\end_inset

A conventional normalization rule is to set 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part C, (ii)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The likelihood function for all observations is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L={\displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \prod_{j=1}^{J}}p_{ij}^{y_{ij}}
\]

\end_inset

and the log-likelihood function is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ln L={\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}{\displaystyle \sum_{j=1}^{J}}y_{ij}\ln\left(p_{ij}\right)
\]

\end_inset

To estimate the parameters, we maximize the log-likelihood function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part C, (iii)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the multinomial logit model, we use the normalization 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}=0$
\end_inset

.
 Then,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{i1}=\frac{1}{1+{\displaystyle \sum_{k=2}^{J}}e^{X_{i}\beta_{k}}}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{ij}=\frac{e^{X_{i}\beta_{j}}}{1+{\displaystyle \sum_{k=2}^{J}}e^{X_{i}\beta_{k}}},\ j\geq2
\]

\end_inset

As a result,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ln\left(\frac{p_{ij}}{p_{i1}}\right)=X_{i}\beta_{j}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial\ln\left(\nicefrac{p_{ij}}{p_{i1}}\right)}{\partial X_{ik}}=\beta_{jk}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{jk}$
\end_inset

 represents the marginal effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{ik}$
\end_inset

 on the log odds ratio.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part C, (iv)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In the conditional logit model, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{ij}=\frac{e^{Z_{ij}\gamma}}{{\displaystyle \sum_{k=1}^{J}}e^{Z_{ik}\gamma}}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, the log odds ratio between two alternaties 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ln\left(\frac{p_{ij}}{p_{ik}}\right)=(Z_{ij}-Z_{ik})\gamma
\]

\end_inset

only depends on the attributes of alternatives in comparison.
 This property of the conditional logit model is called the IIA.
 Consider the example of three choices, traveling in a car, a red bus, and
 a blue bus.
 The relative probability between the car and red bus is most likely to
 change when the alternative blue bus is introduced.
 But, the IIA indicates that the relative probability between the car and
 the red bus will not be affected.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part C, (v)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The assumption of the model that leads to the IIA is 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{ij}\sim\mbox{i.i.d.}$
\end_inset

 The nested logit model avoids this problem.
 A 
\emph on
two-level
\emph default
 nested logit model posits that some alternatives can be grouped together,
 each group including one or more alternatives.
 Decisions are made in two steps: choice of a group and then a choice of
 alternative.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider the Tobit model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=\begin{cases}
y_{i}^{*} & \mbox{if}\ y_{i}^{*}=X_{i}\beta+u_{i}^{*}>0\\
0 & \mbox{if}\ y_{i}^{*}=X_{i}\beta+u_{i}^{*}\leq0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

where strictly exogenous regressors are fully observed, and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is an i.i.d.
 
\emph on
normal
\emph default
 random variable with zero mean and variance 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{+}=y_{i}^{*}>0$
\end_inset

 be the observed positive value of the latent variable.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=X_{i}\beta+u_{i}$
\end_inset

 be the regression equation of the observed censored variable and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{+}=X_{i}\beta+u_{i}^{+}$
\end_inset

 be the regression equation of positive latent variable only.
 Note that notations are a little different from the notations in the lecture
 notes.
 Note also that 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is explicitly assumed to be a normal random variable.
 You must use the notations in this question and also use the notations
 of normal pdf 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi(z)$
\end_inset

 and normal cdf 
\begin_inset Formula $\Phi(z)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Derive 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}^{+})$
\end_inset

, and prove that the OLSE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}^{+}=X_{i}\beta+u_{i}^{+}$
\end_inset

 is biased.
 Which of the Gauss-Markov assumptions is violated?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Derive 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i})$
\end_inset

, and find the marginal effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{ik}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i})$
\end_inset

 and interpret the result.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Explain how to construct the likelihood function, and write the likelihood
 function that is strictly concave with respect to the parameters.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The error terms in the regression equation do not have zero means, and hence,
 OLSE is biased.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(y_{i}^{+}) & = & E(y_{i}^{*}|y_{i}^{*}>0)\\
 & = & E(X_{i}\beta+u_{i}^{*}|X_{i}\beta+u_{i}^{*}>0)\\
 & = & X_{i}\beta+E(u_{i}^{*}|u_{i}^{*}>-X_{i}\beta)\\
 & = & X_{i}\beta+\sigma\frac{\phi\left(-\frac{X_{i}\beta}{\sigma}\right)}{1-\left(-\frac{X_{i}\beta}{\sigma}\right)}\\
 & = & X_{i}\beta+\sigma\frac{\phi\left(\frac{X_{i}\beta}{\sigma}\right)}{\Phi\left(\frac{X_{i}\beta}{\sigma}\right)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(y_{i}) & = & P(y_{i}=0)E(y_{i}|y_{i}=0)+P(y_{i}^{*}>0)E(y_{i}^{*}|y_{i}^{*}>0)\\
 & = & P(u_{i}^{*}>-X_{i}\beta)E(y_{i}^{+})\\
 & = & \Phi\left(\frac{X_{i}\beta}{\sigma}\right)\left[X_{i}\beta+\sigma\frac{\phi\left(\frac{X_{i}\beta}{\sigma}\right)}{\Phi\left(\frac{X_{i}\beta}{\sigma}\right)}\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The marginal effect of 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{ik}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i})$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial E(y_{i})}{\partial X_{ik}}=\frac{\partial P(y_{i}^{+})}{\partial X_{ik}}E(y_{i}^{+})+P(y_{i}^{+})\frac{\partial E(y_{i}^{+})}{\partial X_{ik}}
\]

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 signify the expenditure of household 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 on durable goods.
 The term on the left captures the effect of the regressor on the change
 of the households which have a positive expenditure, either moving from
 positive expenditure to zero expenditure, or vice versa.
 If a household moves from zero to positive expenditure, their expected
 expenditure is 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i}^{+})$
\end_inset

.
 The term on the right captures the effect of the regressor on the change
 in household expenditure on those who had positive expenditure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part C.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $d_{i}$
\end_inset

 be a dummy variable
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
d_{i}=\begin{cases}
1 & \mbox{if}\ y_{i}>0\ (y_{t}^{*}\leq0)\\
0 & \mbox{if}\ y_{i}=0\ (y_{t}^{*}>0)
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

The single likelihood function is either the discrete probability 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{t}^{*}\leq0)$
\end_inset

 where
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
P(y_{t}^{*}\leq0) & = & P(u_{i}^{*}\leq-X_{i}\beta)\\
 & = & P\left(\frac{u_{i}}{\sigma}\leq-\frac{X_{i}\beta}{\sigma}\right)\\
 & = & \Phi\left(-\frac{X_{i}\beta}{\sigma}\right)\\
 & = & 1-\Phi\left(\frac{X_{i}\beta}{\sigma}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

or the density 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{i}^{*})$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t}>0$
\end_inset

 where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(y_{t}^{*})=f(y_{i}-X_{i}\beta)
\]

\end_inset

So, the single likelihood function is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L_{i}(y_{i})=\left[1-\Phi_{i}\right]^{1-d_{i}}\left[\frac{1}{\sigma}\phi\left(\frac{X_{i}\beta}{\sigma}\right)\right]^{d_{i}}
\]

\end_inset

The likelihood function and the log likelihood function for all observations
 are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
L & = & {\displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^{n}}\left[1-\Phi_{i}\right]^{1-d_{i}}\left[\frac{1}{\sigma}\phi\left(\frac{y_{i}-X_{i}\beta}{\sigma}\right)\right]^{d_{i}}\\
\ln L & = & {\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\left(1-d_{i}\right)\ln\left[1-\Phi_{i}\right]+d_{i}\ln\left[\frac{1}{\sigma}\phi\left(\frac{y_{i}-X_{i}\beta}{\sigma}\right)\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Now, let 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha=\nicefrac{\beta}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau=\nicefrac{1}{\sigma}$
\end_inset

.
 Then, the log likelihood function can be rewritten as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ln L={\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\left(1-d_{i}\right)\ln\left[1-\Phi\left(X_{i}\alpha\right)\right]+d_{i}\ln\left[\tau\phi\left(\tau y_{i}-X_{i}\alpha\right)\right]
\]

\end_inset

With these transformations of the parameters, the log-likelihood function
 is now strictly concave with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider a system of two latent variables
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i1}^{*}=X_{i1}\beta_{1}+u_{i1}^{*},\ y_{i2}^{*}=X_{i2}\beta_{2}+u_{i2}^{*},\ i=1,\ldots,n
\]

\end_inset

where error vector 
\begin_inset Formula $(u_{i1}^{*},u_{i2}^{*})$
\end_inset

 is an i.i.d.
 normal random vector with zero mean and covariance matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\Sigma=(\sigma_{ij})$
\end_inset

.
 Regressors are fully observed, but only the sign of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i2}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is observed: 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i2}=1$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i2}^{*}>0$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i2}=0$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i2}^{*}\leq0$
\end_inset

.
 And, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i1}=y_{i1}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is observed only when 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i2}=1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Derive 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{i1})$
\end_inset

 and explain why the LSE of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 from the observed variable 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{i1}=X_{i1}\beta+u_{i1})$
\end_inset

 is biased.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Explain Heckman's two-step estimation procedure.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Explain a potential problem in estimating an equation for 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i1}^{*}$
\end_inset

 (i) when there is no common variable in 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i2}$
\end_inset

 and (ii) when there are common varaibles in 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
E(y_{i1}) & = & E(y_{i1}^{*}|y_{i2}^{*}>0)\\
 & = & E(X_{i1}\beta_{1}+u_{i1}^{*}|X_{i2}\beta_{2}+u_{i2}^{*}>0)\\
 & = & X_{i1}\beta_{1}+E(u_{i1}^{*}|u_{i2}^{*}>-X_{i2}\beta)\\
 & = & X_{i1}\beta_{1}+E\left[\left.\left(\frac{\sigma_{12}}{\sigma_{22}}\right)u_{i2}^{*}\right|u_{i2}^{*}>-X_{i2}\beta\right]\\
 & = & X_{i1}\beta_{1}+\left(\frac{\sigma_{12}}{\sigma_{22}}\right)E(u_{i2}^{*}|u_{i2}>-X_{i2}\beta)\\
 & = & X_{i1}\beta_{1}+\left(\frac{\sigma_{12}}{\sqrt{\sigma_{22}}}\right)\frac{\phi\left(\frac{X_{i2}\beta_{2}}{\sqrt{\sigma_{22}}}\right)}{\Phi\left(\frac{X_{i2}\beta_{2}}{\sqrt{\sigma_{22}}}\right)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The second term in this expression is the source of the bias and inconsistency
 of the least squares estimator of 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

From the mean function 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{1})$
\end_inset

, we write a regresison equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{1}=X_{1}\beta_{1}+\left(\frac{\sigma_{12}}{\sigma_{2}}\right)\frac{\phi(X_{2}\alpha_{2})}{\Phi(X_{2}\alpha_{2})}+\xi_{1}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{1}$
\end_inset

 is heteroskedastic.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{2}$
\end_inset

 is known, we can compute 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{\phi(X_{2}\alpha_{2})}{\Phi(X_{2}\alpha_{2})}$
\end_inset

 and then estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\frac{\sigma_{12}}{\sigma_{2}}\right)$
\end_inset

 by the OLS or the GLS (heteroskedasticity).
 Heckman's two-step procedure is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{2}$
\end_inset

 with probit MLE (setting 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{22}=1$
\end_inset

 since it is not identifiable)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{1}$
\end_inset

 be the OLS or the GLS.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
Part C.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If a common variable is included in 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{2}$
\end_inset

, one may make a false conclusion that the proper equation is nonlinear
 in that variable.
\end_layout

